#!/bin/bash
#
# Run `astro check` with the ANSI colour stripped, for after/compiler/astro.vim.
#
# Two things force this to be a script rather than part of 'makeprg':
#
# 1. `astro check` always colours its diagnostics. It formats them with
#    TypeScript's own colouring routine and emits escapes unconditionally —
#    not only when stdout is a terminal. NO_COLOR, FORCE_COLOR, CI and
#    TERM=dumb were all tried; none of them make any difference. Left in, the
#    escapes sit in front of every %f and nothing in the errorformat matches.
#
# 2. The filter cannot live in 'makeprg'. :Make comes from vim-dispatch, which
#    builds its own command line instead of handing 'makeprg' to a shell the
#    way built-in :make does, and a pipe does not survive that: it is dropped
#    without a word, the output arrives still coloured, and it looks for all
#    the world like the errorformat is at fault.
#
# perl rather than sed because BSD sed, which is what macOS ships, has no \x1b.
#
# Usage: astro-check <project-root>
#   The argument is appended by :Make; see after/compiler/astro.vim.

# The same perl pass also drops everything that isn't a diagnostic: the
# startup banner, the blank lines, the echoed source line with its ~~~~
# underline, and the trailing "Result (N files)" summary. The errorformat's
# %-G catch-all is supposed to handle those, but :Make doesn't honour it —
# vim-dispatch adds every unmatched line to the quickfix list as an invalid
# entry, which is what fills :copen with || noise. Filtering here means the
# list holds diagnostics and nothing else, under :Make and :make alike.
#
# Trade-off: TypeScript occasionally elaborates an error across several
# indented continuation lines, and those are dropped with everything else.
# The first line carries the file, position and summary; coc shows the full
# text inline in the buffer.
#
# Report astro check's exit status, not perl's, so :Make still knows whether
# the check passed. perl always exits 0, so with pipefail the pipeline takes
# astro check's status. (grep would not work here: it exits 1 when nothing
# matches, turning a clean project into a reported failure.)
set -o pipefail

npx astro check --root "$@" 2>&1 |
	perl -ne 's/\e\[[0-9;]*m//g; print if /:\d+:\d+ - (?:error|warning|hint) ts\(/'
